===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[German]]- or [[Austrian]]-style [[sausage]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John W{{quote-gloss|illiam}} Hill|chapter=Food: A Collage of Chemicals|title=Chemistry for Changing Times|edition=2nd|location=Minneapolis, Minn.|publisher=Burgess Publishing Company|year=1975|page=251|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/chemistryforchan0000hill_g0h3/page/251/mode/1up|isbn=0-8087-0836-8|passage=If you pick up a package of liverwurst (German ''wurst'', sausage), you may find that it contains, in addition to liver, nonfat dry milk, salt, corn syrup, flavorings (unspecified), sodium erythrobate, sodium nitrate, sodium nitrite, gelatin, and dextrose. The list reads like a list of chemicals for a laboratory experiment! If you wish to avoid “chemicals” in your food, '''wurst''' is one of the worst things you can buy.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Sammy Tippit, as told to [[w:Jerry B. Jenkins|Jerry Jenkins]]|chapter=With God in Germany|title=Sammy Tippit: God’s Love in Action|location=Chicago, Ill.|publisher=[[w:Moody Bible Institute#Moody Publishers|Moody Press]]|year=1973|year_published=1976|page=117|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sammytippitgodsl0000samm/page/117/mode/1up|isbn=0-8024-7510-8|passage=We had a long walk ahead to Stuttgart. We stopped to eat at a little meat market just outside Augsburg. I bought a loaf of bread and chose a couple of packages of '''wurst'''. It really was good.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardyment Heidi's Alp|page=163|passage=Although the supermarket tannoy was bravely broadcasting ''Rigoletto'', its meat counter was thick with '''wurst''' and its wine shelves loaded with Liebfraumilch.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Berkeley students in cooperation with the {{w|Associated Students of the University of California}}|entry=Bolzano|chapter=The Dolomites and the Northeast|title=Italy on the Loose 1995|series=The Berkeley Guides: The Budget Traveler’s Handbook|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Fodor's|Fodor’s Travel Publications, Inc.]]|year=1994|page=85|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/italyonloose0000unse/page/85/mode/1up|isbn=0-679-02640-1|passage=Sure, the map says it’s Italy, but after walking through the centuries-old market at Piazza Erbe (Obstplatz) and seeing one too many suspiciously Teutonic-looking locals munching '''wurst''', you might think you’ve crossed the Austrian border.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Tom Davis (comedian)|Tom Davis]]|chapter=Incidents and Coincidence: Death Camps, Franken, and the Beatles|title=Thirty-Nine Years of Short-Term Memory Loss: The Early Days of SNL from Someone Who Was There|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Grove Press|year=2009|page=201|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/thirtynineyearso00davi/page/201/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-8021-1880-6|passage=At an open-air flea market, we looked for Nazi memorabilia while munching '''wurst''' and drinking beer.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Dardis McNamee|title=Frommer's Austria
|passage=In summer, you're welcomed into a flower-decked garden set against a backdrop of ancient vineyards. You can fill up your platter with some of the best '''wursts''' and roast meats (especially the delectable pork), along with freshly made salads.}}

